The '''Corporació Hidroelèctrica de Balboa''' (CHB) is a [[state-owned enterprise]] responsible for hydroelectric power generation in [[Balboa]]. It is the oldest and largest electricity generator in the country, operating the network of dams and hydroelectric stations on the [[Yalabirga]] river system. Hydropower accounts for approximately 38 per cent of Balboa's electricity supply.

== History ==

=== Formation (1962) ===

CHB was established in 1962 to oversee the construction of Balboa's first hydroelectric dam, built on [[Llac de la Boira]] in [[Tramuntana]] province with financing from an [[Adonian Community]] loan. The dam, completed in 1964, marked the beginning of domestic electricity generation in Balboa, which had previously been dependent on imported coal and hydrocarbons for its entire power supply.

Following reunification in 1973, CHB was integrated into the national energy system under the Ministry of Energy and Natural Resources. The post-reunification government invested heavily in expanding hydroelectric capacity, constructing several major dams in the Yalabirga highlands during the 1970s and 1980s.

=== Expansion and limits ===

Through the 1970s and 1980s, CHB built the majority of the dams that form the current Yalabirga hydroelectric network. The [[Gran Presa del Nord]], constructed on the southern shore of [[Lake Iwarakí]] in the [[La Serra de Llevant]] comarca of [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] during this period, was among the largest projects undertaken during the expansion. The dam converted the natural lake into a regulated reservoir and became a major source of hydroelectric power for the northern highlands. The company's output grew steadily, and by the early 1990s hydropower supplied more than half of Balboa's electricity.

By the mid-1990s, however, the most suitable dam sites had been exploited, and further expansion faced mounting opposition. Environmental groups and [[Birú people|Birú]] communities, whose traditional lands in the Yalabirga highlands had already been affected by dam construction, opposed additional hydroelectric development on the grounds that it would entail further land destruction, deforestation, and displacement of indigenous communities. The opposition made large-scale hydroelectric expansion politically unfeasible, and the government turned to nuclear power — through the establishment of [[Organització d'Energia Nuclear de Balboa|OENB]] in 1998 — to meet growing demand.

== Operations ==

* '''Headquarters''': [[Sant Bart]], [[Balboa]]
* '''Dams and stations''': Network of hydroelectric dams on the [[Yalabirga]] river system, including the original 1964 dam at [[Llac de la Boira]] in [[Tramuntana]] and the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] on [[Lake Iwarakí]]
* '''Share of electricity supply''': approximately 38% (2026)
* '''Employees''': approximately 3,200 (2026)

CHB operates a network of dams, reservoirs, and hydroelectric stations across the Yalabirga range. The company's output is seasonal, with peak generation during the wet season and reduced output during dry periods. This seasonal variability was one of the key reasons the government pursued nuclear power as a complementary baseload source.

== Ownership ==

CHB is wholly owned by the [[Government of Balboa]]. It operates under the oversight of the Ministry of Energy and Natural Resources and is not listed on any stock exchange.

